# How to cite Anatria3D.
#
# Citation File Format 1.2.0 — https://citation-file-format.github.io
# GitHub reads this to render "Cite this repository", and Zenodo reads it when
# minting a DOI for a release.
#
# `version` and `date-released` describe the release this file ships in, so
# they belong to the release checklist alongside package.json, Cargo.toml and
# tauri.conf.json. A stale version here is worse than none: it would tell a
# reader to cite a build they are not running.
cff-version: 1.2.0
title: Anatria3D
message: If you use this software, please cite it.
type: software

authors:
  - family-names: Kerimov
    given-names: Nurdzhan
    affiliation: Digital Rose
    email: info@digitalrose.org
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0009-0214-3017"

abstract: >-
  A local-first 3D human anatomy atlas for education, with an assistant that
  controls the viewport as it explains — isolating a structure, ghosting a
  layer, marking a pathway — so the explanation and the anatomy are on screen
  together. Structures are named in Terminologia Anatomica (TA2). The
  application runs entirely on the user's machine and holds no patient data.
  It is educational material and is not a medical device.

keywords:
  - anatomy
  - medical education
  - 3D visualisation
  - Terminologia Anatomica
  - female anatomy
  - local-first software
  - open educational resources

# The **concept** DOI, not the version one. Zenodo mints both: this resolves to
# whichever release is newest, while 10.5281/zenodo.21921086 is frozen at
# v0.1.6 and would quietly go stale. Same rule as linking /releases/latest
# rather than a tag.
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.21921085
    description: Concept DOI — always resolves to the latest archived version.

license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: https://github.com/Nurkan1/Anatria-3D
version: 0.2.4
date-released: 2026-09-05

# The anatomical meshes and nomenclature are not ours and are not covered by
# the Apache licence. Anyone citing this work should carry their attribution
# chain too — see NOTICE and public/anatomy/NOTICE.
references:
  - type: data
    title: Z-Anatomy — the libre 3D atlas of anatomy
    notes: Derived from BodyParts3D. Licensed CC BY-SA 4.0.
    authors:
      - name: The Z-Anatomy project
  - type: data
    title: BodyParts3D
    notes: Licensed CC BY-SA 2.1 Japan.
    authors:
      - name: Database Center for Life Science (DBCLS), Japan
  # The female atlas, and a different licence: CC BY 4.0, with no share-alike.
  - type: data
    title: Human Reference Atlas 3D Reference Organ Library
    notes: >-
      Reference organ set ref-organ/united-female v1.5. Licensed CC BY 4.0.
      The female atlas in this software is derived from it.
    url: https://humanatlas.io/
    authors:
      - name: HuBMAP Consortium
      - name: National Institutes of Health (NIH)
  - type: data
    title: The Visible Human Female
    notes: >-
      The dataset the Human Reference Atlas female organs are reconstructed
      from.
    authors:
      - name: U.S. National Library of Medicine
